# A route sign: a post and a board.
# Y is up, +Z is the way the creature faces, one world unit is one map tile.
# The lowest vertex sits on y = 0 and the model is centred on x = 0 and z = 0,
# so the game places it by its feet.
#
# Faces are wound so that (c - a) x (b - a) points out of the solid, which is
# what tools/obj2cpp.py bakes as the face normal. Get it backwards and the
# model still draws, it just goes flat ambient dark.
#
# Eyes are not modelled. They are two depth tested dots in the 2D layer, which
# is a pixel each instead of eight triangles.
#
# About 0.98 units tall, 20 triangles.
# The pale face is on +Z, so a sign is rotated to face its reader the
# same way a creature is rotated to face its opponent.

mtllib sign.mtl

# post, 8 triangles
v 0.045 0.620 0.045
v -0.045 0.620 0.045
v -0.045 0.620 -0.045
v 0.045 0.620 -0.045
v 0.055 0.000 0.055
v -0.055 0.000 0.055
v -0.055 0.000 -0.055
v 0.055 0.000 -0.055
usemtl sign_post
f 1 5 6 2
f 2 6 7 3
f 3 7 8 4
f 4 8 5 1

# board, 12 triangles
v 0.360 0.980 0.050
v -0.360 0.980 0.050
v -0.360 0.980 -0.050
v 0.360 0.980 -0.050
v 0.360 0.500 0.050
v -0.360 0.500 0.050
v -0.360 0.500 -0.050
v 0.360 0.500 -0.050
usemtl sign_face
f 9 13 14 10
usemtl sign_edge
f 10 14 15 11
usemtl sign_back
f 11 15 16 12
usemtl sign_edge
f 12 16 13 9
f 9 10 11 12
f 16 15 14 13
